**Root cause:** disk is attached to a **running** instance — GCP blocks image creation from an in-use disk unless you either stop the instance or force it.

| Option | Downtime | Risk | Command |
|---|---|---|---|
| A. Stop instance, then create image | None to prod (this VM isn't serving traffic) | Zero — clean, consistent snapshot | Stop → create → done |
| B. `--force` while running | None | Possible filesystem inconsistency (low risk here since it's a bare OS with no active writes) | One command, no stop needed |

**Recommendation: Option A** — this instance isn't serving any traffic (it's just your Packer source base), so stopping it costs nothing and guarantees a clean image.

```bash
gcloud compute instances stop clean-ubuntu-26092026 \
  --project=pg-ae-n-app-125609 \
  --zone=me-central2-a
```

Then:
```bash
gcloud compute images create clean-ubuntu-26092026-img \
  --project=pg-ae-n-app-125609 \
  --source-disk=clean-ubuntu-26092026 \
  --source-disk-zone=me-central2-a
```

Verify:
```bash
gcloud compute images describe clean-ubuntu-26092026-img --project=pg-ae-n-app-125609 --format="value(status)"
```
